<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C9.S1 — Code parameters and bounds</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C9.S1 — Reading [[n,k,d]]</p></div><p>A stabilizer code has parameters \([[n,k,d]]\) with \(d={@d@}\).</p><p>(a) Give the number of arbitrary single-qubit errors it can correct, in terms of <code>d</code> (use <code>floor</code>).</p><p>\(t=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the number of independent stabilizer generators, in terms of <code>n</code> and <code>k</code>.</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) The quantum Singleton bound reads \(n-k\ge \alpha(d-1)\). Give \(\alpha\).</p><p>\(\alpha=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(t=\lfloor (d-1)/2\rfloor\). For \(d={@d@}\) this is \({@ta1@}\). A distance-\(d\) code <em>detects</em> \(d-1\) errors but only <em>corrects</em> half that many, since two correctable errors must map the code space to distinguishable subspaces.</p><p>(b) The code space is the joint \(+1\) eigenspace of \(n-k\) independent generators, each halving the dimension: \(2^{n}/2^{n-k}=2^{k}\).</p><p>(c) \(\alpha=2\): the quantum Singleton bound is \(n-k\ge2(d-1)\), twice the classical one, because both bit and phase errors must be corrected. Codes meeting it with equality are called quantum MDS; the \([[5,1,3]]\) code is an example.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
