  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C9.S2 — Below and above threshold</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C9.S2 — The threshold theorem, quantitatively</p></div><p>For a distance-\(d\) surface code, \(p_L\approx A\,(p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2}\) with \(d\) odd.</p><p>(a) Give \(p_L\) for \(A=1\), \(p_{\mathrm{th}}=10^{-2}\), \(p=10^{-3}\) and \(d={@d@}\) (exact expression, no decimals).</p><p>\(p_L=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Concatenating \(\ell\) levels of a distance-3 code gives \(p_L=p_{\mathrm{th}}(p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{e(\ell)}\). Give the exponent \(e(\ell)\) in terms of <code>l</code>.</p><p>\(e(\ell)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) Give the number of physical qubits per logical qubit for a distance-\(d\) surface code (leading order, in terms of <code>d</code>).</p><p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The exponent is \((d+1)/2\); with \(p/p_{\mathrm{th}}=10^{-1}\) this gives \(p_L=10^{-(d+1)/2}={@ta1@}\).</p><p>(b) Each concatenation level squares the ratio, so \(e(\ell)=2^{\ell}\): the suppression is doubly exponential in the number of levels, and only \(\mathrm{polylog}\) levels are needed for any target — the content of the threshold theorem.</p><p>(c) \(d^{2}\) (or \(2d^{2}-1\) counting ancillas, depending on the layout). At \(p/p_{\mathrm{th}}=1/10\) and a target of \(10^{-12}\), \(d\approx23\), so roughly \(500\)–\(1000\) physical qubits per logical qubit — before accounting for magic state factories, which typically dominate the total footprint.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
