  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C9.S3 — Stabilizer arithmetic and the Steane code</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C9.S3 — Counting states and syndromes</p></div><p>The Steane code is \([[7,1,3]]\).</p><p>(a) Give the number of independent stabilizer generators.</p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the number of distinct non-trivial syndromes available, and compare with the number of weight-1 Pauli errors on 7 qubits. Enter the ordered pair <code>[syndromes, errors]</code>.</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) For a general \([[n,1,3]]\) code, the quantum Hamming bound requires \(2^{n-1}\ge 1+3n\). Give the smallest \(n\) satisfying it.</p><p>\(n=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(n-k=7-1=6\).</p><p>(b) Six generators give \(2^{6}=64\) syndromes, of which \(63\) are non-trivial. There are \(3\times7=21\) weight-1 Pauli errors, so the syndromes are far from exhausted — the Steane code is not perfect. (It is, however, CSS and admits transversal Clifford gates, which matters more in practice.)</p><p>(c) \(2^{n-1}\ge 1+3n\): at \(n=5\), \(16\ge16\) holds with equality, so \(n=5\). That is the \([[5,1,3]]\) perfect code, which saturates the bound — every non-trivial syndrome corresponds to exactly one correctable error.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
